@BurntSushi Yes you're correct, the type I was wrapping did not implement Debug - it probably could have but I chose not to.  This is a rough paraphrase of the code I had:

```rust
struct Shared {
    ptr: AtomicPtr<_>,
    index: AtomicUsize,
}

// the recv side has the owned copy, and would have a custom Debug implementation
pub struct RecvSide {
    shared: Arc<Shared>,
}

// the send side I was hoping could be simplified by using #[derive]
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct SendSide {
    shared: Weak<Shared>,
}
```

I intentionally didn't want to `#[derive(Debug)]` on the Shared item because the contents are implementation detail in my library, and I'm not personally a fan of the Debug implementations for Atomics (that opinion is not related to this PR).  I suppose I could have manually implemented Debug for Shared so that it could have been derived for the public-facing types - I guess didn't think of that at the time - but I still think if there's no conceivable plan to rely on the `T: Debug` in `Weak<T>`, it doesn't need to retain the bound.

I do think this warrants an FCP.

I think if we're going to have behavior this specific (and potentially unexpected), we should document it.

