> I think that is not the issue here

Okay. I don’t ask because I necessarily thing that would be an issue. I’m asking because a general mechanism for _allowing_ different behavior at compile-time vs. runtime, e.g. a macro
```rs
compile_time_vs_run_time! {
    // if at compiler time:
    {
        // some code here
    }
    // if at run time:
    {
        // some code here
    }
}
```
that can be used in a `const fn` and allows arbitrary code (without the restrictions of `const` evaluation) in its “at run time” branch. Then, for anyone that needs it _now_, a custom `const_panic` macro or `<Result as ResultExt>::const_unwrap` method, etc, could be offered in an external crate, without any need for the standard library’s `panic` or `Result::unwrap` to _indefinitely_ support questionable fall-back logic with under-constrained trait bounds.

Admitted, with such a crate then _every_ case of compile-time evaluation of those panics would result in _not_ printing the values involved, even for simple `std` types like numbers or `str`, but it’d still enable people to keep their run-time diagnostics good.

<hr>

If there’s concerns that a general mechanism for different run-time vs. compile-time behavior is unwanted, there’s also an option of a `compile_time_vs_run_time_panic` macro that’s the same as explained above, but requires that both branches eventually panic (e.g. by requiring type `!` and disallowing any use of `return`/`continue`/`break`).

